Lec 28
 
 
 
Flux of \vec{F} through surface is
 

\iint_S \vec{F} \cdot \hat{n} \, dS

 
 
 
- If S is graph of z = f(x,y)
 

\hat{n} \, dS = \pm \langle -f_x, -f_y, 1 \rangle \, dx \, dy

 
 
 

\pm \vec{U} \times \vec{V} = \Delta S \cdot \hat{n}

 
\vec{U}: from (x,y,f(x,y))
to (x+\Delta x,y,f(x+\Delta x,y))
\approx f(x,y) + \Delta x \cdot f_x
 

\vec{U} \approx \langle \Delta x, 0, f_x \Delta x \rangle = \langle 1, 0, f_x \rangle \Delta x

 
\vec{V} \approx \langle 0, \Delta y, f_y \Delta y \rangle = \langle 0, 1, f_y \rangle \Delta y
 
 
 
(with \hat{n} upwards)
 

\hat{n} \, \Delta S = \vec{U} \times \vec{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\vec{i} & \vec{j} & \vec{k} \\
1 & 0 & f_x \\
0 & 1 & f_y
\end{vmatrix} \Delta x \Delta y = \langle -f_x, -f_y, 1 \rangle \Delta x \Delta y

Lec 28-2
 
 
 
Example:
\vec{F} = z \hat{k} through portion of paraboloid z = x^2 + y^2 above unit disk, normal upwards (should be >0)
 

\begin{align*}
\iint_S \vec{F} \cdot \hat{n} \, dS
&= \iint \langle 0, 0, z \rangle \cdot \langle -2x, -2y, 1 \rangle \, dxdy \\
&= \iint_S z \, dxdy = \iint_S (x^2 + y^2) \, dxdy \\
&= \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^1 r^2 \cdot r \, dr d\theta = \frac{\pi}{2}
\end{align*}

 
 
 
More generally: given parametric description of S
 

S: \begin{cases}
x = x(u,v) \\
y = y(u,v) \\
z = z(u,v)
\end{cases}

 
\langle x,y,z \rangle = \vec{r} = \vec{r}(u,v)
 

\hat{n} \, dS = \pm \left( \frac{\partial \vec{r}}{\partial u} \times \frac{\partial \vec{r}}{\partial v} \right) du dv

 
 
 
- If we know a normal vector \vec{N} (not necessarily unit) to the surface S:
1. Plane: ax + by + cz = d \implies \vec{N} = \langle a,b,c \rangle
2. S given by equation: g(x,y,z) = 0 \implies \vec{N} = \nabla g
 
Surface element: \Delta A = \Delta S \cos \alpha, where \cos \alpha = \frac{\vec{N} \cdot \hat{n}}{|\vec{N}|}
 
So:
 

\Delta S = \frac{1}{\cos \alpha} \Delta A = \frac{|\vec{N}|}{\vec{N} \cdot \hat{n}} \Delta A

 

\hat{n} \, dS = \frac{|\vec{N}| \hat{n}}{\vec{N} \cdot \hat{n}} \Delta A = \pm \frac{\vec{N}}{\vec{N} \cdot \hat{k}} \Delta A

 

\hat{n} \, dS = \pm \frac{\vec{N}}{\vec{N} \cdot \hat{k}} \, dxdy

